# Git hooks must keep LF endings on every platform.
#
# ⚠ A `#!/bin/sh` script checked out with CRLF fails as "bad interpreter: No
# such file or directory" — the shell reads the trailing CR as part of the
# interpreter path. Windows clones default to CRLF on checkout, so without this
# the pre-commit hook would silently stop running for anyone on Windows: the
# worst failure mode for a guard, since it looks exactly like "no violations".
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Shebang scripts must keep LF too.
#
# ⚠ Git stores these as LF, but core.autocrlf=true rewrites them to CRLF on
# Windows checkout — and while node(1) tolerates a shebang line whose
# newline is preceded by a carriage return,
# vitest's esbuild transform rejects it as "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected
# token". The failure is invisible on Linux CI and hits only Windows clones,
# which is the worst shape: `npm test` disagrees with itself by platform.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf

# ...and the SAME rule for the other two shebang-bearing file types, which
# this file has now had to learn three times, one type over each time.
#
# ⚠ HONEST SCOPE: unlike the .js/.mjs rules above, this fixes a LATENT
# risk, not an observed break. Nothing invokes `./scripts/x.py` or `./x.sh` --
# every .py is run as `<interpreter> <file>`, which ignores the shebang, and
# all 111 of these files are mode 100644, so none can be executed directly at
# all. Their shebangs are decorative today, exactly as the 17 .js shebangs were
# (see reference_ci_and_io_test_timeouts).
#
# ⚠ It is still worth pinning, and it is free: git already stores all 111 as
# LF, so this changes no bytes today. What it buys is that a Windows checkout
# followed by a renormalising commit cannot quietly put CRLF into the index --
# and if anything ever DOES exec one of these directly, `#!/usr/bin/env python3`
# with a trailing CR fails on Linux as "bad interpreter" while working fine in
# Git Bash, which msys makes tolerate it. That is this file's recurring shape:
# a break that is invisible on the machine you are typing on.
*.py text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf

# ⚠⚠ AND JSON, BECAUSE A GENERATED FILE IS COMPARED BYTE FOR BYTE.
#
# `scripts/buildMnOsaAuditBranch.mjs --check` re-derives its JSON from the OSA
# source and compares it to the committed file to prove the file is not stale.
# The generator writes LF; a Windows checkout rewrote the committed copy to CRLF;
# so the guard reported STALE on a file that was byte-identical in content. A
# check that fails on the line endings of its own checkout is worse than no
# check, because the obvious "fix" is to regenerate and commit the CRLF version,
# which then fails for everyone on Linux.
#
# ⚠ EXACTLY the failure the Census fixture rule below was added for (#139),
# and it recurred here two commits after *.py and *.sh were pinned. The lesson
# this file keeps re-learning is to pin the CLASS, not the file that just broke:
# any file whose BYTES are asserted by a checker or a hash needs an eol rule.
#
# ⚠ Measured before adding: all 102 tracked .json files are already stored
# LF (or have no newlines at all), so this changes NO bytes today.
*.json text eol=lf

# Verbatim data fixtures must keep the SOURCE's line endings.
#
# WARNING: `tests/fixtures/census/co-est2024-sc-slice.csv` is a byte extract of
# the Census PEP county file, which uses LF endings. Without this rule a Windows
# checkout rewrites it to CRLF, and the parity test that re-reads the real source
# file then matches NONE of its 51 lines: it compares CR-terminated fixture lines
# against a source it split on CRLF-or-LF, so every line differs by one trailing
# carriage return. Observed exactly that way -- green on Linux CI, where the
# source file is absent and the test skips, and red on the first Windows clone
# that had the cache file. Same shape as the shebang rules above: CI and a
# developer machine disagreeing by platform.
tests/fixtures/census/*.csv text eol=lf

# A rescued document whose VERBATIM-ness is asserted by a recorded hash must keep
# the bytes that hash was taken over.
#
# ⚠ `docs/inform-pillar-treasury-tracker-spec.md` carries its original sha256 in
# its own provenance header, so a Windows checkout rewriting LF to CRLF would
# make the file disagree with the hash printed inside it — the claim and the bytes
# would part company silently. Exactly the failure the Census fixture rule below
# was added for, one file type over.
docs/inform-pillar-treasury-tracker-spec.md text eol=lf
